Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||08-21-2001||10:26 PM||||To expand on what Cliff said, copyright is extended automatically to any original creation, be it writing, painting, photography, etc. Copyright is ownership, and  licesnse to distribute. You may give or sell all or some rights to someone else (like a publisher), or you may retain all rights to reproduce (as a wedding photographer might) to control copying quality and, of course, profit.<P>Some works may not be copywritten to their true originator, such as in a "work for hire" agreement, i.e. say I worked for Cliff. I draw a salary (Or an assignment fee) to write articles about Fieros for his website. The copyright does not belong to me, as I am creating this work for Cliff in exchange for his paying me. I have no right to be disgruntled when Cliff makes big dough (or not) from the sweat of my brow. I only can negotiate better next time. (The names in this example have been changed to protect the innocent. I'm sure if I had really ment Cliff Pennock, and we really had an arrangement like this, he would, of course, reward me handsomely.)||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||08-21-2001||11:35 PM||||That's a decent quick example of work for hire. Like everything else involving "intelectual property rights," it's allot more complicated than that. <P>There are conditions where even WFH reverts to the original author. You've also got wierd issues with "free lance" and other works, as many media outlets are now trying to settle over internet publishing of freelance and advertising actors work.<P>I've spent a bunch of time digging around the LOC site about this... The more I figure out, the more questions I end up with. This whole napster thing has been a b_tch to follow. The free lance and advertising actors stuff has also been fun. These last two are why many radio stations stopped streaming.||||reg||1
